---
import Command from "../../components/Command.astro";
import StoryBars from "../../components/StoryBars.astro";
import StaticPage from "../../layouts/StaticPage.astro";
import { viewerHref } from "../../lib/story-links";
import { SERIES } from "../../lib/story-series";

const home = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/ui\/$/, "");
const repository = "https://github.com/simonvanlierde/brightway-benchmarks";
const single = "bench_lca.SingleScore.time_first_score";
const multi = "bench_lca.MultiScores.time_scores";
const carlo = "bench_lca.MonteCarlo.time_iterations";
const scipySource = "https://github.com/scipy/scipy/blob/v1.18.1/scipy/sparse/linalg/_dsolve/linsolve.py";
const link = (group: string, bench: string, ...params: string[]) => viewerHref(home, group, bench, params);

// One score: benchmarks/findings.toml, "The solver sets the time for one score".
const oneScore = link("bw2calc-conda", single, "ecoinvent", "LCA");
const pipScore = link("bw2calc", single, "ecoinvent", "LCA");
// 30 products: published results of the latest bw2calc-conda run, in the viewer's MultiScores view.
const thirty = (variant: string) => link("bw2calc-conda", multi, "ecoinvent", "30", variant);
// Real ecoinvent: docs/findings.md, the solver table under "On the real ecoinvent 3.12 matrix".
const realFindings = `${home}findings/#umfpacks-speed-depends-on-its-blas-and-its-refinement-step`;
const check = `python -c "import bw2calc as bc; print('PyPardiso' if bc.PYPARDISO else 'UMFPACK' if bc.UMFPACK else 'SuperLU')"`;
---

<StaticPage
  mainClass="story"
  current="solver-choice"
  title="Your solver choice can change a Brightway score's time by 5 times or more · Brightway Benchmarks"
  description="On the real ecoinvent 3.12 matrix, the solver work behind one Brightway score takes 3.66 s with SciPy's SuperLU, 1.48 s with PyPardiso or 0.66 s with scikit-umfpack from conda-forge."
  canonical="https://simonvanlierde.com/brightway-benchmarks/stories/solver-choice/"
  image="story-solver-choice.png"
  imageAlt="Three bars for factorizing the real ecoinvent 3.12 matrix: UMFPACK from conda-forge 0.66 seconds, PyPardiso 1.48 seconds, SuperLU 3.66 seconds."
>
      <h1>Your solver choice can change a Brightway score's time by 5 times or more</h1>
      <p class="lede">SuperLU takes 3.66 s to factorize the real ecoinvent 3.12 matrix, scikit-umfpack from conda-forge 0.66 s: 5.5 times as long. On real ecoinvent a single score saves about 3 s of factorizing, and the saving counts per Monte Carlo draw and per scenario: about 50 minutes over 1,000 draws. On the synthetic benchmark database the whole first score takes 4.98 s against 1.03 s.</p>
      <p>Every Brightway score ends by solving one large sparse linear system: one equation per product, with almost every coefficient zero. For ecoinvent, the large background database most LCA studies use, that is 26,533 equations. The solver does that arithmetic, and bw2calc picks it from what you have installed. All times use one thread; PyPardiso can use several and is not measured with them.</p>

      <aside class="install" aria-labelledby="install">
        <h2 id="install">What to install</h2>
        <p>First check which solver you have:</p>
        <Command command={check} />
        <p>If it prints SuperLU, install one of these:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>conda:</strong> scikit-umfpack, and don't install PyPardiso. bw2calc uses PyPardiso whenever it is installed, so it runs UMFPACK only without it.<Command command="conda install -c conda-forge scikit-umfpack" /></li>
          <li><strong>pip or uv:</strong> PyPardiso. It needs Intel's MKL, which PyPI has for Linux x86_64, 64-bit Windows and, up to MKL 2023.2.2, Intel Macs.<Command command="pip install pypardiso" /><Command command="uv add pypardiso" />scikit-umfpack from PyPI (<code>pip install scikit-umfpack</code> or <code>uv add scikit-umfpack</code>, which compiles on install) is a little slower than PyPardiso.</li>
          <li><strong>Mac with Apple silicon:</strong> PyPardiso won't install, so take scikit-umfpack from conda-forge.</li>
          <li><strong>bw_timex on Linux x86_64 or 64-bit Windows:</strong> bw_timex requires PyPardiso there, so you get PyPardiso. Removing it breaks bw_timex, so keep it.</li>
          <li><strong>Many products in one go (<code>MultiLCA</code>):</strong> PyPardiso. If you only need the scores, <code>from bw2calc import FastScoresOnlyMultiLCA</code> with UMFPACK is faster still.</li>
        </ul>
        <span class="sr" role="status" data-copy-status></span>
      </aside>

      <h2>Real ecoinvent</h2>
      <p>Factorizing the technosphere matrix (splitting it into two triangular matrices that are quick to solve) is 96 to over 99% of the solver's time for one score. These are the factorization times on the ecoinvent 3.12 cut-off matrix, on one thread.</p>
      <StoryBars
        caption="Factorizing real ecoinvent 3.12, one thread"
        href={realFindings}
        linkText="The numbers in the findings"
        rows={[
          { label: "scikit-umfpack from conda-forge (UMFPACK, on OpenBLAS)", value: 0.66, series: SERIES.umfpack },
          { label: "PyPardiso (PARDISO, on MKL)", value: 1.48, series: SERIES.pypardiso },
          { label: "scikit-umfpack from PyPI (UMFPACK, on reference BLAS)", value: 1.78, series: SERIES.umfpack },
          { label: "Nothing extra (SciPy's SuperLU)", value: 3.66, series: SERIES.superlu },
        ]}
      />

      <p>The charts below come from a pinned benchmark machine, on one thread, and each links to its benchmark's full history. They use a synthetic database with ecoinvent's size and shape. A whole score there takes 1.4 to 1.6 times as long as the factorization above, mostly because the synthetic matrix takes longer to factorize than the real one, and the solvers come in the same order.</p>

      <h2>One score</h2>
      <p>bw2calc's <code>LCA</code> class builds the matrices, factorizes the technosphere, solves it and characterizes the result.</p>
      <StoryBars
        caption="Time to the first score"
        href={oneScore}
        rows={[
          { label: "scikit-umfpack from conda-forge (UMFPACK, on OpenBLAS)", value: 1.03, series: SERIES.umfpack },
          { label: "PyPardiso (PARDISO, on MKL)", value: 2.22, series: SERIES.pypardiso },
          { label: "Nothing extra (SciPy's SuperLU)", value: 4.98, series: SERIES.superlu },
        ]}
      />
      <p>With nothing extra installed, bw2calc uses SciPy's SuperLU, the slowest bar. With PyPardiso installed, bw2calc uses it even when scikit-umfpack is there too. So the fastest bar needs scikit-umfpack and no PyPardiso.</p>
      <p>Where scikit-umfpack comes from matters too. UMFPACK does most of its work through BLAS, the library of basic matrix routines that NumPy also uses. conda-forge links it to OpenBLAS, a fast BLAS. PyPI has only scikit-umfpack's source, so pip compiles it against whatever BLAS you have.</p>
      <p>The pip build measured here runs on reference BLAS, the plain textbook version. It is a little slower than PyPardiso on both databases: 2.54 s against 2.30 s for PyPardiso from PyPI on the synthetic one (<a href={pipScore}>the pip build's history</a>), and 1.78 s against 1.48 s on the real matrix.</p>

      <h2>Thirty products at once</h2>
      <p><code>MultiLCA</code> scores 30 products against three impact methods in one call.</p>
      <StoryBars
        caption="MultiLCA, 30 products"
        href={thirty("MultiLCA")}
        rows={[
          { label: "PyPardiso (PARDISO, on MKL)", value: 2.75, series: SERIES.pypardiso },
          { label: "Nothing extra, or scikit-umfpack (SciPy's SuperLU)", value: 5.40, series: SERIES.superlu },
        ]}
      />
      <p>Here scikit-umfpack makes no difference. Without PyPardiso, <code>MultiLCA</code> passes all 30 demands to SciPy's <code>spsolve</code> at once, as one matrix with 30 columns. <code>spsolve</code> hands the work to UMFPACK only for a single right-hand side, one demand vector (<a href={`${scipySource}#L233`}>SciPy's source</a>). For more, it uses SuperLU. PyPardiso solves all 30 itself and is about twice as fast.</p>
      <p>If you only need the scores, <code>FastScoresOnlyMultiLCA</code> factorizes the matrix once and then solves the products separately (one at a time with UMFPACK, 50 per call with PyPardiso), so UMFPACK is back in front: in bw2calc 3.1.0, 1.63 s, against 2.68 s for PyPardiso (<a href={thirty("forward")}>the forward-direction history</a>). It needs PyPardiso or UMFPACK and refuses to run on SuperLU.</p>

      <h2>Monte Carlo, and the class you call</h2>
      <p>Each Monte Carlo draw has new matrix values, so the solver starts over and its time counts once per draw. On the synthetic database, ten draws with <code>LCA</code> and PyPardiso take 21.6 s (<a href={link("bw2calc-conda", carlo, "ecoinvent", "10", "LCA")}>the Monte Carlo history</a>).</p>
      <p>The bw2calc class matters too. <code>JacobiGMRESLCA</code> doesn't factorize: it improves a guess step by step until the result is within a tolerance, so its score is close, not exact. In bw2calc 3.1.0 it gives one score in about 130 ms, with a relative error of 5.6e-9, and ten draws in 0.65 s (<a href={link("bw2calc-conda", carlo, "ecoinvent", "10", "JacobiGMRESLCA")}>its history</a>).</p>
      <p>That is on the synthetic matrix. bw2calc's own docs report a 20% error on one real ecoinvent process, so check the result before you rely on it. <code>IterativeLCA</code> factorizes once, then iterates; UMFPACK does the one factorization, and ten draws take 0.76 s, on the synthetic database too.</p>

      <details class="setup">
        <summary>How this was measured</summary>
        <ul>
          <li>A pinned benchmark machine, Linux x86_64, in a container, on one efficiency core. BLAS and OpenMP run on one thread: <code>OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1</code>, <code>MKL_NUM_THREADS=1</code>, <code>OMP_NUM_THREADS=1</code>.</li>
          <li>Python 3.13 from conda-forge, bw2calc 3.1.0, NumPy 2.5.3, SciPy 1.18.1, PyPardiso 0.4.7 with MKL 2026.1.0, and scikit-umfpack 0.4.2 (SuiteSparse 7.10.1) on OpenBLAS 0.3.34. The pip build is scikit-umfpack 0.4.2 from PyPI. The one-score times are the same in every bw2calc release from 2.0 to 3.1.0, except PyPardiso from PyPI, which takes 0.09 s longer from 3.0.0 on (<a href={pipScore}>the release history</a>).</li>
          <li>A synthetic database with 26,533 products, like ecoinvent 3.12 cut-off.</li>
          <li>Real ecoinvent: the ecoinvent 3.12 cut-off technosphere matrix, 26,533 rows and 338,195 nonzeros, on one efficiency core of the pinned machine, one thread. <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/scripts/solver_factorize.py`}><code>scripts/solver_factorize.py</code></a> times one factorization of each solver (median of 3) through bw2calc 3.1.0's <code>spsolve</code> and <code>lu_solver</code>, with SciPy 1.18.1, PyPardiso 0.4.7 and scikit-umfpack 0.4.2 from PyPI and from conda-forge. A performance core gives 1.7 to 2 times shorter times in the same order.</li>
          <li>One score: <code>LCA(demand=&#123;0: 1&#125;, data_objs=[inventory, method])</code>, then <code>lci()</code> and <code>lcia()</code>. Thirty products: <code>MultiLCA(demands=..., method_config=..., data_objs=...)</code>, then <code>lci()</code> and <code>lcia()</code>, or <code>FastScoresOnlyMultiLCA(..., direction="forward")</code> and <code>next(lca)</code>. Monte Carlo: <code>LCA(..., use_distributions=True, seed_override=1)</code>, then ten <code>next(lca)</code> calls.</li>
          <li>Each timed call has a fresh setup that has already solved once, so imports stay out of the time. Each time is asv's median of 4 to 10 calls. Every score is checked against a reference outside the timed call.</li>
          <li>Source: <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/benchmarks/bench_bw2calc/bench_lca.py`}>the benchmarks</a>, <a href={scipySource}>SciPy's <code>spsolve</code></a> and the <code>JacobiGMRESLCA</code> docstring in bw2calc 3.1.0 for the 20% error (a demand for “market for waste polystyrene”).</li>
        </ul>
      </details>

      <p><a href={home}>Setup</a> has install sets that passed their checks on each platform. <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/docs/faster.md`}>Make your Brightway faster</a> lists other install choices and calls that change the time, each with its measured gain. The <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/docs/method.md`}>method</a> describes the database and how noisy the timings are.</p>
    </StaticPage>

<script is:inline>
  // Copy buttons stay hidden without this script: the commands are still there to select.
  for (const button of document.querySelectorAll("[data-copy]")) {
    button.hidden = false;
    button.addEventListener("click", async () => {
      const status = document.querySelector("[data-copy-status]");
      try {
        await navigator.clipboard.writeText(button.previousElementSibling.textContent);
        status.textContent = "Copied the command";
      } catch {
        status.textContent = "Copy failed; select the command instead";
      }
    });
  }
</script>

<style>
  .install,
  .setup {
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    padding: 4px 20px;
    margin: 24px 0;
  }
  .install {
    background: var(--accent-wash);
    border-color: var(--accent);
    padding: 8px 22px 18px;
  }
  :global(.story) .install h2 {
    margin-top: 12px;
  }
  .install :global(.install-command) {
    background: var(--page);
    margin: 8px 0;
  }
  .install li,
  .setup li {
    margin: 12px 0;
  }
  .install code,
  .setup code {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .setup summary {
    cursor: pointer;
    font-weight: 600;
    padding: 12px 0;
  }
</style>
